#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158470 :tutorial/content "Given an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n\\}$ of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathcal{B}\\},$ there is an easy way to find the components of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in S$ with respect to this basis. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFurthermore, if the basis is *orthonormal*, then we know that $\\mathbf{q}_i\\cdot\\mathbf{q}_i=1$. So, the formula for each component is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "67f07988-4aba-56fb-b9e7-aef39a3de89b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4302 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158471 :tutorial/content "Given any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and any subspace $W$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we can always write $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of a vector from $W$ and a vector from $W^{\\perp}.$ This fact is known as the **orthogonal decomposition theorem.** We can state it formally as follows:\n\n> *Let $W$ be a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then each $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ can be written uniquely in the form\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> where $\\mathbf{y} \\in W$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\in W^{\\perp}.$*\n\n> *Moreover, since $\\mathbf y$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf x$ onto the subspace $W,$ if $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1\\,\\dots,\\mathbf{u}_p\\}$ is any orthogonal basis of $W,$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{W}\\,x \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{1}}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{2}}\\,x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{p}}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{1}}{{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}}{u}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{2}}{{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}}{u}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{p}}{{u}_{p}\\cdot {u}_{p}}{u}_{p}.\\end{aligned}\n> $$" :tutorial/id #uuid "789dc38d-c1e0-5bdc-9f01-5940371a326b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4304 :tutorial/title "The Orthogonal Decomposition Theorem"}]}
